#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087452 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087877 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9c49e068-198f-59ab-8835-7018b02d7d0c" :knowledge-point/title "Transforming First-Order Linear ODEs Using Integrating Factors"}] :question/id #uuid "1e3c0253-226d-596c-a831-7c68ec76b21c" :question/math-academy-id "e-21741" :question/problem "Consider the following first-order linear ODE for the function $y = y(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x} + 2y = 2e^{-2x}\n$$\n\nWrite the equation in the form $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(y I(x) \\right) = I(x)Q(x),$ where $I(x)$ is an integrating factor." :question/worked-solution "This equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} + P(x)y = Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith $P(x) = 2$ and $Q(x) = 2e^{-2x}.$ We proceed to find an integrating factor $I(x)$ using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int 2\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply our differential equation through by $I(x) = e^{2x}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2x}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y) & ={e}^{2x}\\cdot 2{e}^{-2x} \\\\ {e}^{2x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2{e}^{2x}\\cdot y & ={e}^{2x}\\cdot 2{e}^{-2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rewrite the left hand side as $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(y I(x)\\right){:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{e}^{2x}) & ={e}^{2x}\\cdot 2{e}^{-2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087453 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087878 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6bbeb4f6-23b2-586c-8cb9-9742cce5a9a9" :knowledge-point/title "Solving First-Order Linear ODEs Using Integrating Factors"}] :question/id #uuid "89446fd7-75c2-5da5-bd97-469100f3ca7b" :question/math-academy-id "e-21742" :question/problem "Find the general solution of the following differential equation using the method of integrating factors.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x} + \\dfrac {3} {x} y = \\dfrac{\\cos x}{x^2}, \\qquad x \\gt 0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "This equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} + P(x)y = Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith $P(x) = \\dfrac{3}{x}$ and $Q(x) = \\dfrac{\\cos x}{x^2}.$ We proceed to find an integrating factor $I(x)$ using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (3/x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})} \\\\  & ={x}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply our differential equation through by $I(x) = x^3$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{3}{x}y) & ={x}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {x}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+3{x}^{2}\\cdot y & =x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rewrite the left hand side as $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(y\\cdot I(x)\\right)$ and carry out the integration:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{x}^{3}) & =x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{x}^{3})\\text{d}x & =\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y{x}^{3} & =\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this integral, we integrate by parts. \n\n$$\n\\int uv'\\,\\textrm d x = uv - \\int u' v\\,\\textrm d x\n$$\n\nLet $u = x, u' = 1, v' = \\cos x,$ and $v = \\sin x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y{x}^{3} & =\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y{x}^{3} & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y{x}^{3} & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C \\\\ y & =\\frac{x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{C}{{x}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087454 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087879 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "01c5809f-cb10-5192-b646-13036813137d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving First-Order Initial Value Problems Using Integrating Factors"}] :question/id #uuid "09d09b0b-2132-59b2-9eb9-ae0748f09252" :question/math-academy-id "e-21743" :question/problem "Solve the following initial value problem using the method of integrating factors.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x} + 3x^2y = 9x^2, \\qquad y(0) = 1\n$$" :question/worked-solution "This equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} + P(x)y = Q(x),\n$$\n\nwith $P(x) = 3x^2$ and $Q(x) = 9x^2.$\n\nWe compute an integrating factor using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int 3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equation in standard form by $I(x) = e^{x^3}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{{x}^{3}}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+3{x}^{2}y) & ={e}^{{x}^{3}}\\cdot 9{x}^{2} \\\\ {e}^{{x}^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+3{x}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{3}}\\cdot y & =9{x}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{3}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{e}^{{x}^{3}}) & =9{x}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x,$ making use of the substitution $u = x^3{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{e}^{{x}^{3}})\\text{d}x & =\\int 9{x}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y{e}^{{x}^{3}} & =\\int 3{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\ y{e}^{{x}^{3}} & =3{e}^{u}+C \\\\ y{e}^{{x}^{3}} & =3{e}^{{x}^{3}}+C \\\\ y & =3+C{e}^{-{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the general solution. To find the value of $C$ corresponding to the specific solution, we apply the initial condition $y(0) = 1$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =3+C{e}^{-{0}^{3}} \\\\ 1 & =3+C \\\\ C & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny = 3 - 2e^{-x^3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435867 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087877 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9c49e068-198f-59ab-8835-7018b02d7d0c" :knowledge-point/title "Transforming First-Order Linear ODEs Using Integrating Factors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b978ec0-c1dc-593e-85ec-c512eb8b8bba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x|)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x|\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435832} {:answer/id #uuid "1da376ec-fadc-58b2-9050-98c3a0e3289d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(y{x}^{2})={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435835} {:answer/id #uuid "39544074-ae02-530d-9e3a-2a0b0340a99a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(y{e}^{2x})={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435838} {:answer/id #uuid "97ed5649-7d28-5597-8785-b502fbbd5161" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(y\\cdot 2x)=2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435845} {:answer/id #uuid "a6707d7d-a941-50a8-9de7-4cedeafd40fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(y{e}^{{x}^{2}})={e}^{{x}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435847}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a6707d7d-a941-50a8-9de7-4cedeafd40fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(y{e}^{{x}^{2}})={e}^{{x}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435847} :answer-field/id #uuid "7bf5e5ce-2a24-563a-8eed-d37940cab8bf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435863}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "73fd6309-052f-5dd1-8e4c-82525a25d454" :question/math-academy-id "q-335274" :question/problem "Consider the following first-order linear ODE for the function $y=y(x):$\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+2xy=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$\n\nWrite the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(yI(x))=I(x)Q(x),$ where $I(x)$ is an integrating factor." :question/worked-solution "This equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith $P(x)=2x$ and $Q(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ We proceed to find an integrating factor $I(x)$ using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,dx} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int 2x\\,dx} \\\\  & ={e}^{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply our differential equation through by $I(x)={e}^{{x}^{2}}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\cdot (\\frac{dy}{dx}+2xy) & ={e}^{{x}^{2}}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {e}^{{x}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx}+2x{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\cdot y & ={e}^{{x}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rewrite the left hand side as $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y\\cdot I(x)):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}(y{e}^{{x}^{2}}) & ={e}^{{x}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311587 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087878 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6bbeb4f6-23b2-586c-8cb9-9742cce5a9a9" :knowledge-point/title "Solving First-Order Linear ODEs Using Integrating Factors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24c590c3-891b-511b-9e8d-1e0317eac036" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309024} {:answer/id #uuid "c4250262-bc93-5a7e-a34a-2623ae9d7561" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310322} {:answer/id #uuid "c82f0da4-6a92-5001-89a2-66f34618a0ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{3/x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310355} {:answer/id #uuid "ee20a83a-6373-5864-bf59-eeaad3c5fb28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310646}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c4250262-bc93-5a7e-a34a-2623ae9d7561" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310322} :answer-field/id #uuid "26833fad-1674-5264-a99a-1ab167ee5815" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310888} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34e87914-2491-5ea9-986c-2fa933b9f42e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{x}+C}{{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309150} {:answer/id #uuid "93802e8a-2438-526b-9013-756c85306841" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}({e}^{x}+C)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309928} {:answer/id #uuid "cc68d1a8-cb75-5053-8d7d-c7bbccf33bcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{x}+C}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310385} {:answer/id #uuid "ce5f5849-731e-572b-9350-7951b8593415" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{3}({e}^{x}+C)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310405}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "34e87914-2491-5ea9-986c-2fa933b9f42e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{x}+C}{{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309150} :answer-field/id #uuid "842dfce6-3596-5e08-aa3d-f4ed3e02c6ff" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311159}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "039312c7-7497-5736-bf10-d497d1c61328" :question/math-academy-id "q-335412" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{3}{x}y=\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{3}},\\,x>0.\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$ An integrating factor for this equation is $I(x)=$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The general solution is given by $y(x)=$ {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "This equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith $P(x)=\\frac{3}{x}$ and $Q(x)=\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{3}}.$ We proceed to find an integrating factor $I(x)$ using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,dx} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (3/x)\\,dx} \\\\  & ={e}^{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})} \\\\  & ={x}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply our differential equation through by $I(x)={x}^{3}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{3}{x}y) & ={x}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{3}} \\\\ {x}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+3{x}^{2}\\cdot y & ={e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rewrite the left hand side as $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y\\cdot I(x))$ and carry out the integration:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dx}(y{x}^{3})\\,dx & =\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y{x}^{3} & ={e}^{x}+C \\\\ y & =\\frac{{e}^{x}+C}{{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088144 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087879 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "01c5809f-cb10-5192-b646-13036813137d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving First-Order Initial Value Problems Using Integrating Factors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42485b83-5931-53b1-8e35-f51001ba1f8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435839} {:answer/id #uuid "9ffccaf5-41e6-5e01-a737-ef5c5c68550a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435846} {:answer/id #uuid "d15343f4-d7a9-5b07-861f-21158d88cd11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-{e}^{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435855} {:answer/id #uuid "e8b3ee9d-6980-58f8-a0f9-f8fb1fe2f5f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-{e}^{-{x}^{2}/4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435856} {:answer/id #uuid "f6d4fe97-8780-56b9-9818-a1be81f11d20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435859}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e8b3ee9d-6980-58f8-a0f9-f8fb1fe2f5f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-{e}^{-{x}^{2}/4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435856} :answer-field/id #uuid "3037a011-e9f1-5dab-aa7d-6ffe3d4faaec" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085509} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f03f76b8-d043-505d-8492-b243b8108a8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085516} {:answer/id #uuid "8d866b2c-596b-59ce-a796-aa22c8b178ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{{x}^{2}/4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435843} {:answer/id #uuid "ae550e0c-1776-5b97-ab81-e46cb204df85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435850} {:answer/id #uuid "fc50ddf9-0597-5079-a434-4f4d589089a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435860}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8d866b2c-596b-59ce-a796-aa22c8b178ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{{x}^{2}/4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435843} :answer-field/id #uuid "af75cec9-5a94-548e-88fa-c37d26f071ef" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085515}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8d9c75e9-1ee8-5d41-904e-f9bb375cf039" :question/math-academy-id "q-335808" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{x}{2}\\cdot y=x,\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$ An integrating factor for this equation is $I(x)=$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The solution to the initial value problem is $y(x)=$ {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "This equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x),\n$$\n\nwith $P(x)=\\frac{x}{2}$ and $Q(x)=x.$\n\nWe compute an integrating factor using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (x/2)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{{x}^{2}/4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equation in standard form by $I(x)={e}^{{x}^{2}/4}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{{x}^{2}/4}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{x}{2}y) & ={e}^{{x}^{2}/4}\\cdot x \\\\ {e}^{{x}^{2}/4}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{x}{2}{e}^{{x}^{2}/4}\\cdot y & =x{e}^{{x}^{2}/4} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{e}^{{x}^{2}/4}) & =x{e}^{{x}^{2}/4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x,$ making use of the substitution $u=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{e}^{{x}^{2}/4})\\text{d}x & =\\int x{e}^{{x}^{2}/4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y{e}^{{x}^{2}/4} & =\\int 2{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\ y{e}^{{x}^{2}/4} & =2{e}^{u}+C \\\\ y{e}^{{x}^{2}/4} & =2{e}^{{x}^{2}/4}+C \\\\ y & =2+C{e}^{-{x}^{2}/4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the general solution. To find the value of $C$ corresponding to the specific solution, we apply the initial condition $y(0)=1$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =2+C{e}^{-{0}^{2}/4} \\\\ 1 & =2+C \\\\ C & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=2-{e}^{-{x}^{2}/4}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435868 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087879 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "01c5809f-cb10-5192-b646-13036813137d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving First-Order Initial Value Problems Using Integrating Factors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d921686-f16a-5dba-8a57-bf18cea65de9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-2x}(1+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435834} {:answer/id #uuid "21b21254-3e18-5695-9053-f6ca9ce7117b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{-2x}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435837} {:answer/id #uuid "ae129e42-b937-59e5-976e-b7f0a9bd2526" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2x}(5-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435849} {:answer/id #uuid "cf7b3997-dd21-54f1-8bae-4ea74ec54405" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2x}(3+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435854} {:answer/id #uuid "f1b016d8-b939-5406-bcfa-caab3f97d6bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435857}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ae129e42-b937-59e5-976e-b7f0a9bd2526" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2x}(5-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435849} :answer-field/id #uuid "872a01bb-a58c-517b-8a38-5d170114d390" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435864} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0249902f-f26e-5a08-91d2-72b3ea108e58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435831} {:answer/id #uuid "12775c73-4492-5f55-9c3e-64f7ff76cc43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435833} {:answer/id #uuid "89b946b2-2ff0-52e9-9809-36bbebc85401" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435841} {:answer/id #uuid "b2373707-12f6-5094-9104-60d956b3f32f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435851}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0249902f-f26e-5a08-91d2-72b3ea108e58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435831} :answer-field/id #uuid "db6d7e71-81cf-5a3b-b7fa-5481d846b976" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435865}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "eb095d57-a547-5e39-b061-eaeaefb98858" :question/math-academy-id "q-335830" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2y={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\\,y(0)=4\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$ An integrating factor for this equation is $I(x)=$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The solution to the initial value problem is $y(x)=$ {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "This equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x),\n$$\n\nwith $P(x)=-2$ and $Q(x)={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).$\n\nWe compute an integrating factor using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (-2)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equation in standard form by $I(x)={e}^{-2x}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-2x}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2y) & ={e}^{-2x}\\cdot {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {e}^{-2x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2{e}^{-2x}\\cdot y & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{e}^{-2x}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{e}^{-2x})\\text{d}x & =\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y{e}^{-2x} & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C \\\\ y & ={e}^{2x}(C-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the general solution. To find the value of $C$ corresponding to the specific solution, we apply the initial condition $y(0)=4$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & ={e}^{2(0)}(C-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\ 4 & =C-1 \\\\ C & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{2x}(5-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435866 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087878 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6bbeb4f6-23b2-586c-8cb9-9742cce5a9a9" :knowledge-point/title "Solving First-Order Linear ODEs Using Integrating Factors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21b04f8f-b31f-5e1d-8a4e-18caced2e9c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435836} {:answer/id #uuid "8bb32a9a-ce7c-5dfe-90c1-5f241762ceee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435842} {:answer/id #uuid "cbf9a5e7-9387-5f18-9a2c-3d8174e2d646" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435852} {:answer/id #uuid "f669873b-95d4-589e-957f-2bb522268738" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435858}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "21b04f8f-b31f-5e1d-8a4e-18caced2e9c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435836} :answer-field/id #uuid "054a463e-8111-5ef5-af3f-4d0babd67edf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435861} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "856f41fb-43dc-578b-9457-c64453f94455" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+\\frac{C}{{x}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435840} {:answer/id #uuid "96612919-8760-51a3-a0ec-f59a5ea738c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+\\frac{C}{{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435844} {:answer/id #uuid "a91ab2dc-5a5e-503c-8f55-0ebae59165d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{C}{{x}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435848} {:answer/id #uuid "cceac854-6bb0-51ac-8a27-c1be3b7727f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{C}{{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435853}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "856f41fb-43dc-578b-9457-c64453f94455" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+\\frac{C}{{x}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435840} :answer-field/id #uuid "658b2e4e-a8cb-5de8-9ced-3ee2b6b96997" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435862}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "536cae39-f031-5c30-a8b0-a9b62b8632a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-339139" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{4}{x}y=5,\\,x>0.\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options below:\n\n$\\,$ An integrating factor for this equation is $I(x)=$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The general solution is given by $y(x)=$ {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "This equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith $P(x)=\\frac{4}{x}$ and $Q(x)=5.$ We proceed to find an integrating factor $I(x)$ using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,dx} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (4/x)\\,dx} \\\\  & ={e}^{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{4})} \\\\  & ={x}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply our differential equation through by $I(x)={x}^{4}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{4}{x}y) & ={x}^{4}\\cdot 5 \\\\ {x}^{4}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4{x}^{3}y & =5{x}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rewrite the left hand side as $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y\\cdot I(x))$ and carry out the integration:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dx}(y{x}^{4})\\,dx & =\\int 5{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y{x}^{4} & ={x}^{5}+C \\\\ y & =x+\\frac{C}{{x}^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348294 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087877 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9c49e068-198f-59ab-8835-7018b02d7d0c" :knowledge-point/title "Transforming First-Order Linear ODEs Using Integrating Factors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "205e27b5-4aef-53eb-8cf0-e7365d5f4c57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{x}^{2})={x}^{2}{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345637} {:answer/id #uuid "7510063a-6cf7-5663-bf6a-5afc7e2ed0eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{e}^{x})={e}^{x}{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346382} {:answer/id #uuid "8483449c-5ecd-5c41-92e1-0ded8b81ec2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{e}^{-x})={e}^{-x}{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346505} {:answer/id #uuid "b9c267a8-32e4-55c8-8af3-388216c0c46a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(yx)=x{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346964} {:answer/id #uuid "cf856d8b-bb28-5fc8-bf77-c77ea36fa1c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\cdot {e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347167}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b9c267a8-32e4-55c8-8af3-388216c0c46a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(yx)=x{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346964} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b3a3107-9d3a-5db2-9a74-94dd3b070895" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347795}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7613e649-714b-58e4-9ccc-7a0b944dc47c" :question/math-academy-id "q-79818" :question/problem "Consider the following first-order linear ODE for the function $y=y(x):$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot y={e}^{x},\\,x>0\n$$\n\nWrite the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(yI(x))=I(x)Q(x),$ where $I(x)$ is an integrating factor." :question/worked-solution "This equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith $P(x)=\\frac{1}{x}$ and $Q(x)={e}^{x}.$ We proceed to find an integrating factor $I(x)$ using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (1/x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply our differential equation through by $I(x)=x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot y) & =x\\cdot {e}^{x} \\\\ x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y & =x{e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rewrite the left hand side as $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y\\cdot I(x)):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(yx) & =x{e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
